modeling data relationships is one of  the single most important concerns for  an app developer in this episode I'm  going to show you how to build a  five-star review system from scratch  using the new fire stored document  database and angular my goal is to give  you a good foundation for building  many-to-many relationships in a no  sequel back-end environment the reason I  chose a five-star review system for this  episode is because the underlying  concepts can be applied to many  different features such as Facebook  Likes reddit votes Instagram hearts and  many other concepts similar to those one  of the most important best practices  with firestore is to create big  collections and small documents to build  this feature we're going to have three  collections in the root of the database  so this first collection is a user's  collection and the only attribute on the  document itself is the display name I  won't have time to cover user  authentication in this video but if  you're new to my channel make sure to  check out my previous firestore Roth  video to get up to speed on that so back  to the database our primary goal here is  to create a relationship between users  and movies through the Stars collection  so if you look at our movies document it  just has the image title and plot of the  movie if we jump over to the Stars  collection you'll see that's where all  the relational data is cat  each star document will save the movie  ID as well as the user ID and the value  of the rating and then because a user  can only post one review for each movie  I've also created a custom ID here to  ensure that there's no accidental data  duplication so we create the document ID  by concatenating that movie ID to the  user ID separated by an underscore and  you'll see this more once we get into  angular if you come from a sequel  background this is very similar to a  many-to-many through relationship so now  I'm jumping into my angular project I'm  assuming you have angularfire to version  5 installed what I'm going to be doing  today is to creating a very generic  middleman service that can connect  relationships between any two documents  to focus on the relationship aspect of  this feature I'm going to just hard code  a user document and movie document  directly in the app component so I'll  set up a variable for the user document  and movie document which are both  angular firestore document types and  then we'll also set another variable for  the user and the movie as an observable  then during ng on  we'll go ahead and load the documents so  in this case I'm just going to hard code  a user ID in here but in the real world  you would get this from an off service  or some other off mechanism and it'll do  the exact same thing for a movie  document but I'd like to point out that  these two documents never contain any  information about the other party it's  all handled through the middleman star  document that we're going to create  later then to turn these references into  observables we call value changes on  each of the documents we can get the  movie ID or user ID on the angularfire  document itself so we can set up a  getter here and then on the angularfire  store document we can call ref ID more  than anything setting up the getter just  helps clean up the code once we get to  the HTML so inside the app component  HTML I'm going to unwrap the movies  observable and I do that by just calling  movie async and then set it as a  template variable of M with that we can  display a title and display the image  URL as well as the movie plot and we'll  come back to this later after we create  the star review component but before we  do that we're first going to build our  service so run ng generate service star  and make sure to specify the app module  the goal of our service is to  communicate with firestore to build that  middle relationship in the star  collection so after importing  angularfire store i'm going to create an  interface here just to reinforce what a  start is so a star is just going to be  the user ID movie ID and then a value  for the star which brings us from 0.5 to  5 from there we can add angularfire  store to the constructor with that done  I want to show you why I have stars as a  root collection and not a sub collection  under users or movies so let's say we  wanted to get all of the movies that a  user has starred we could do that by  calling collection stars then use the  query callback with where to filter them  by the user ID firestore will  automatically index by the user ID so  this will be a fast query even if it's a  massive collection and then in this case  here we want to return the observable of  this query so we just call value changes  on it now the reason it's a root  collection is because we want to query  stars by their movie ID as well so we  can do the exact same thing just  watching out the user ID for the movie  ID if stars were nested as a sub  collection we'd be able to make one of  these queries but not both of them the  last thing we need from our service is  to set the actual relationship so we'll  create a method here that takes a user  ID movie ID and value of the star review  so we'll go ahead and set that to a  JavaScript object type to our star  interface then we can use this data to  also create a custom ID for our document  so I'm going to set that up as user ID  underscore movie ID this technique will  ensure that you don't have any data  duplication assuming you have unique  user IDs and movie IDs it also provides  a quick way to query a star document if  you already have a movie ID and user ID  somewhere else in your code so the only  thing left to do is make a reference to  this path and then call set with star  data that's what we need for now but you  might also want to add a delete method  if you want users to be able to remove  their past stars that's it for the  service now we can move on to the star  review component so after it's been  generated let's go ahead and first add  it to the app component so we're going  to pass it a movie ID and a user ID from  the parent component if you want to  learn more about this check out my  sharing data with components video but  all we're doing here is passing the  movie ID and user ID strings from the  parent to the child and we'll set that  up with the input decorator here in the  type script in just a second inside the  star review type script we'll go ahead  and import the service as well as the  input decorator from angular core then  we can declare our user ID and movie ID  variables with the decorator this just  tells angular to allow these variables  to be passed indirectly through the HTML  before we go any further let's look a  little closer at how this app works so  we have a current user here on the left  and then a movie on the right and if a  movie has no reviews it just says not  reviewed here the user can then hover  over the stars and when they click it it  will actually update the data in  firebase and it will also calculate the  average rating among all users that have  starred this movie so the next step will  be to set up by a variable for the Stars  observable then I'm going to set a  separate observable for the average  rating and we can use our star service  during ng on a net to retrieve the  actual observable list so in this case  we want to show all of the movie stars  so we just called it movie stars with  movie ID then we can use the rxjs map  operator to break this down into an  average rating after a calling map we're  going to have an observable array of  objects  so first we'll map these objects down to  just their number values in other words  the value that users have rated them and  then with that array of numbers we can  then reduce it down to its average this  code might look kind of ugly but this is  the standard way that you would get the  average of an array in plain JavaScript  and if that array is empty we're just  going to say not reviewed that takes  care of the observables but now we need  an event handler that will update  firestore when the user clicks on the  star icon so we already have the method  set up in the service so we just have to  pass the event handler a value and then  it will use the user ID movie ID and  that value to update firestore so let's  jump over to the HTML and put this all  together so for the average rating we  can just call async and we're good to go  it's already been mapped down to the  value that we want to show other users  that have starred a movie we can loop  over the star observable with the async  pipe and then just display some data  about that particular star so we'll say  this user ID gave this movie X amount of  stars so now we can actually build that  form where the user updates their star  rating a lot of this is CSS that I'm not  going to cover but you can get the full  code at the lesson link in the  description so we set up an NG container  here which basically allows you to loop  over something without affecting the Dom  in this case I'm just going to loop over  the integers of 5 through 1 I set up  this loop so they only have to create  two input fields rather than 10 so the  first inputs going to represent a full  star so what we can do is pass the  number down to our start handler so if  the user clicks the fifth star it'll  send a 5 star review up to firestorm and  then we need to set up an ID which is  different for each one so we'll just say  star plus whatever number we're looping  over and these are just HTML radio  buttons with some fancy CSS styling  each input also has a label and it's  important that the four attribute  matches the ID on the input exactly to  create a half star we can duplicate this  process we just have to subtract 0.5  from each number so when we call the  start handler will subtract 0.5 then we  also have different CSS styles on the  half star so that's why we have them  separated as two different inputs like  this the CSS to make this look good on  the front end is actually based on a  code pin so I'll have a link for that in  the description and you can also get the  full code at angular firebase com  so if we check it out on the front end  you can see we have the movie here with  just one average rating of 2.5 if we had  another 5 star rating that average  changes in real-time to 3.75 then if we  go ahead and add another review this  time with firestore pulled up you can  see that the values are being saved in  the back end and real-time as well  that's it four star ratings with angular  and firestorm if this video helped you  please like and subscribe and if you  want to take your app to the next level  with exclusive pro con tent consider  becoming a pro member at angular  firebase com  thanks for watching and I'll see you  next time  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

